The graphics and audio stack — Linux and Windows differ here
------------------------------------------------------------

On Linux these are system shared libraries: present on the machine, linked at run
time, and NOT redistributed by this project. They are Cairo (LGPL 2.1 / MPL 1.1),
FreeType (FTL / GPLv2 dual licence, used under the FTL), fontconfig (MIT-style) and
libX11 (MIT).

NAMp Rack additionally links JACK's client library (LGPL 2.1, dynamically linked,
and likewise not redistributed here), as does NAMp Rations' development standalone.
The VST3 bundle does NOT link it, and the release script checks that rather than
asserting it; nothing on Windows links it at all.

rations.lv2's UI half links the same graphics stack the VST3 editor does, for the
plain reason that it IS that editor. Its DSP half links none of it — it draws
nothing — and the release script checks that too.

On Windows there is no system copy of any of them, so the Windows releases
STATICALLY LINK and therefore redistribute them. That changes the licence character
of the shipped binaries, so they are named here in full, with the versions
scripts/build-win-deps.sh pins:

  cairo 1.18.4       https://cairographics.org/
                     LGPL 2.1-or-later OR MPL 1.1 (dual).
  pixman 0.44.0      https://cairographics.org/     MIT.
  FreeType 2.13.3    https://freetype.org/          FTL OR GPLv2 (dual).
  libpng 1.6.48      http://libpng.org/pub/png/     libpng/zlib licence.
  zlib 1.3.1         https://zlib.net/              zlib licence.

  fontconfig is NOT part of the Windows build. It is linked on Linux but never
  called — there is not one Fc* call anywhere in this tree — and FreeType opens the
  bundled .ttf files by path, so the Windows build simply drops it.

  Windows audio is ASIO with WASAPI as the fallback, and WinMM for MIDI. WASAPI and
  WinMM are part of the operating system; ASIO is a Steinberg SDK and has its own
  section below.

  FreeType: the FTL is elected, not the GPLv2 option. The FTL requires this credit
  to appear in the documentation of any product using it, so here it is verbatim:

      Portions of this software are copyright (c) 2024 The FreeType Project
      (www.freetype.org).  All rights reserved.

  FreeType is built with one modification, made by scripts/build-win-deps.sh rather
  than carried as a patch: its meson.build defines DLL_EXPORT on Windows without
  testing default_library, which makes a STATIC build export the whole FreeType API
  from whatever links it. The build script removes that define. No FreeType source
  file is changed.

  cairo and the LGPL: cairo is linked statically into the Windows binaries, which
  LGPL 2.1 section 6 permits provided the recipient can relink the work against a
  modified cairo. That condition is met by publication rather than by a written
  offer: this project's complete corresponding source is published, the exact cairo
  version and the exact configuration used to build it are in
  scripts/build-win-deps.sh in this same repository, and the whole program rebuilds
  from that source with one cmake invocation. Anyone may substitute their own cairo
  and rebuild. No cairo source file is modified.

  WHICH OF CAIRO'S TWO ARMS IS ELECTED DEPENDS ON THE BUILD, and for the GPLv3
  Windows Rack it is not a free choice. cairo offers LGPL 2.1-or-later OR MPL 1.1.

    * The Windows ASIO build of NAMp Rack is GPLv3, so it ELECTS LGPL 2.1. MPL 1.1
      is not GPL-compatible and would make that binary undistributable; LGPL 2.1
      section 3 expressly permits applying the ordinary GPL to a copy of the
      library, and says that where a version later than 2 has appeared "you can
      specify that version instead", which is what reaches GPLv3.
    * Every other build — Linux, both plug-ins, a WASAPI-only Windows Rack — is
      MIT, and either of cairo's arms serves it.

  So the MPL arm is still available to this project; it is simply not available to
  the one binary that carries ASIO. A future change that moved the ASIO build onto
  MPL 1.1 would be a licence breach rather than a preference.

